WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
I'm not going to dive into this too much, but there are a few important concepts and syntax

00:06.000 --> 00:11.000
that I would like you to be familiar with.

00:11.000 --> 00:16.000
When you work with files, there are different modes for opening them.

00:16.000 --> 00:17.000
Don't recognize them.

00:17.000 --> 00:18.000
Sorry, sorry.

00:18.000 --> 00:21.000
Don't memorize them.

00:21.000 --> 00:27.000
Instead, check out this cheat sheet for when you need it and forget it.

00:27.000 --> 00:30.000
It's kind of useless knowledge.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
The important one, though, is our, which is open for reading.

00:35.000 --> 00:37.000
That is the default.

00:37.000 --> 00:43.000
So if you don't pass in a file mode, that's the one that it's going to be.

00:43.000 --> 00:46.000
There are other file modes, like,

00:46.000 --> 00:49.000
Open for writing, but truncate the file.

00:49.000 --> 00:52.000
There's a text mode.

00:52.000 --> 00:56.000
Generally, you'll probably only be working with our or W.

00:57.000 --> 01:00.000
Or occasionally A, which means open this file.

01:00.000 --> 01:04.000
I would like to write to it, but if that file already exists,

01:04.000 --> 01:08.000
append to the end of it instead of overwriting it.

01:08.000 --> 01:14.000
So opening a file and Python looks something like this.

01:14.000 --> 01:16.000
You call open on it.

01:16.000 --> 01:20.000
Remember that will open a file and read mode by defaults.

01:20.000 --> 01:25.000
If you want to open a file for writing, but replace any existing file,

01:25.000 --> 01:30.000
use mode W, or if you want to open a file for reading and writing,

01:30.000 --> 01:33.000
but append to the end of a file.

01:33.000 --> 01:37.000
If it already exists, use mode A.

01:37.000 --> 01:44.000
Now, the thing about files is you want to call close on any open file that you have

01:44.000 --> 01:49.000
to release the handle that your program has on that file back to the operating system.

01:49.000 --> 01:52.000
Otherwise, you're locking it up.

01:53.000 --> 01:58.000
And if your program crashes before you happen to call close,

01:58.000 --> 02:01.000
you might end up in some pretty weird states.

02:01.000 --> 02:07.000
So Python has some shortcuts syntax called a context manager.

02:07.000 --> 02:12.000
And a context manager is like taking a block of code that depends on some resource,

02:12.000 --> 02:14.000
like a file.

02:14.000 --> 02:21.000
And there's some stuff that happens under the hood when you use this syntax,

02:21.000 --> 02:26.000
this keyword with, that will then close the file for you,

02:26.000 --> 02:33.000
and also take care of any cleanup if this happens to throw an exception.

02:33.000 --> 02:37.000
So if you are familiar with other programming languages,

02:37.000 --> 02:42.000
it's similar to a try and finally block, but it's a lot cleaner to look at.

02:42.000 --> 02:48.000
So you can think of context managers as code that just automatically provisions a resource

02:48.000 --> 02:53.000
before your code runs, and then clean the up afterward.

02:53.000 --> 02:59.000
And that open function that we saw earlier, that works as a context manager.

02:59.000 --> 03:02.000
And so opening a file can look like this.

03:02.000 --> 03:06.000
And then we don't have to call close on that file every time,

03:06.000 --> 03:09.000
and litter our code with open closed open closed,

03:09.000 --> 03:14.000
we can use this with keyword and know that that closing is happening for us.

